{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788221475,"nonce":"spypTS2jX57x4zTTm/Fa/VGsciI=","message":"FIX ROUND on branch fix/3.5 (merged from main, clean). The audit's findings were\nre-verified at source before acting; one was wrong, and it is named below.\n\nCORRECTED — DESIGN.md carried a FACTUALLY FALSE claim, and the verb was built on\nit. The 3.5 decisions entry said `quest-dismiss`'s third widget state \"calls\n`QuestManager.Remove`, which is destructive\". It does not.\n`RimWorld/MainTabWindow_Quests.DoDismissButton`'s historical branch, verbatim:\n\n    if (selected.Historical)\n    {\n        selected.hiddenInUI = true;\n        SoundDefOf.Tick_High.PlayOneShotOnCamera();\n        Select(null);\n        return;\n    }\n\nA HIDE. `QuestManager.Remove` is never called from that window at all — its only\nvanilla callers are `Verse/DebugActionsQuests` and\n`RimWorld/QuestPart_SubquestGenerator`. ONLY THE TOOLTIP KEY says delete:\n`string key = (selected.Historical ? \"DeleteQuest\" : (selected.dismissed ?\n\"UnDismissQuest\" : \"DismissQuest\"));`. Reading that key as a destructive call is\nwhat made the verb refuse historical quests with a `historical` gate. The hide is\nreproduced as `mode:\"hide\"`, and since nothing in the game ever clears\n`hiddenInUI`, `dismissed:false` on a historical quest refuses with `no-unhide`\nrather than inventing an un-hide no player has.\n\nTWO REAL EFFECTS WERE MISSING from the same button. It is a TOGGLE\n(`selected.dismissed = !selected.dismissed;`), and it PROPAGATES ONE LEVEL:\n`foreach (Quest subquest in selected.GetSubquests()) subquest.dismissed =\nselected.dismissed;`. The verb wrote the parent flag only, producing a\nparent/subquest split no player can reach through the widget.\n`RimWorld/QuestUtility.GetSubquests` walks the plain\n`QuestManager.questsInDisplayOrder` list for `parent == quest` — direct children\nonly, no write-on-read. Both are now reproduced and REPORTED; omitting\n`dismissed` toggles, passing it sets, and `mode` says which ran.\n\nSECOND WAIVER DECLARED. The same DESIGN entry said `Dialog_NodeTree\n.InteractiveNow` is \"the only widget gate in this spec that is [waived]\". Also\nfalse. `Verse/Dialog_NodeTree`'s constructor sets `closeOnCancel = false` and\n`Verse/Window.OnCancelKeyPressed` closes only `if (closeOnCancel)`, so NO PLAYER\nROUTE clears a node tree without pressing an option — and `dialog-dismiss`\ncloses it anyway. That stays, because the Scope demands an esc-equivalent that\nalways works and 1.7 proves an unanswerable force-pausing window halts every\nadvance at 0 ticks. It is now recorded as waiver 2 in DESIGN.md and at the verb,\nwith the cost published in the verb's own `note`: dismissing a `ChoiceLetter_*`\nnode tree without choosing SKIPS the option's `action`, which is where\n`Find.LetterStack.RemoveLetter(this)` lives, so the LETTER SURVIVES and\n`LetterStackUpdate` can re-open it.\n\nTRADE GATES NOW FAIL CLOSED. Five gate reads had a permissive default behind a\nbare `catch { }` — `traderDismissed`, `CanTradeWith`, `TraderWillTrade`,\n`Interactive`, `DoesTraderHaveEnoughSilver`. The last is why the family was\nfixed: `traderHasSilver = true` on a throwing modded `ITrader` skipped the\n`trader-short-funds` refusal and went straight to `TryExecute`. All refuse with\n`gate-unreadable` now. `trade-start` also gained gate 0: an explicit\n`negotiator` went straight to `Dev.PawnArg` and skipped every filter, so\n`FloatMenuMakerMap.ShouldGenerateFloatMenuForPawn` is reproduced.\n`comms-call`'s ship path now sends `PawnRelationUtility\n.Notify_PawnsSeenByPlayer_Letter_Send` — not presentation; it is how the agent\nlearns a relative is for sale.\n\nTHE ACCEPTANCE SUITE ran nine broken checks and could not run on this box at\nall. Eight call sites passed a computed value where `$Env` belongs (five\nsilently GREEN asserting nothing, three spuriously RED), and 4.8g read\n`data.ticks` where `TimeDriver.BuildData` emits `ticks_elapsed` — the proof that\nun-wedging restored ticking could only ever fail. Fixed, plus a PHASE-0 SHAPE\nCONTRACT using a `HasKey`/`has_key` predicate distinct from `eq(..., None)`, a\ndevMode probe that SKIPS rather than failing four phases later, and staging\ncalls that assert they were accepted. `accept/3.5-dialog-verbs.py` is added as\nthe POSIX twin (same check numbers); the `.ps1` is kept for BORGES.\n\nTHE AUDIT WAS WRONG ON ONE POINT, for the record: it listed `2.5a` as \"two\nsites\". It is one. Eight computed-value sites plus 4.8g's wrong key is nine\nbroken checks, which is the right total by a different decomposition.\n\nBuild: `dotnet build -c Release`, 0 errors, 0 warnings, all 18 op strings in the\nassembly. The DLL is NOT committed — the orchestrator rebuilds on main. No verb\nwas run against a bench; everything above is static verification against\n`rimworld-tools/Info/decompiled/RimWorldBase/`, and the suite's dig paths are\nonly provable on a live run.","files":null}]}